from common import lesson, lab, cite, run, code

LESSONS = [
lesson(6,2,'arrays-and-shapes','NumPy arrays: shape, dtype, indexing, slicing, views',
 'An array is a block of numbers with a shape and a dtype. Everything you will do with activations starts with reading those two attributes.',
 '''<h2 id="three-attributes">Shape, dtype, and the question each answers</h2>
<p><code>a.shape</code> is a tuple: one size per axis, outermost first. <code>a.ndim</code> is its length and <code>a.size</code> the product. <code>a.dtype</code> is the element type, which decides memory, precision, and which operations are allowed together. For an activation tensor the shape reads as a sentence, <code>(batch, seq, d_model)</code>: how many prompts, how many positions each, how many numbers per position. Say it that way every time.</p>
'''+run('python3 labs/examples/arrays.py')+'''
<h2 id="indexing">Indexing and slicing, one axis at a time</h2>
<p><code>a[1, 2]</code> is one element: an index on each axis. <code>a[1]</code> is row 1, shape <code>(4,)</code>: one axis consumed, the rest kept. <code>a[:, 2]</code> is column 2, also <code>(4,)</code> dropped to <code>(3,)</code>: a slice keeps an axis, an integer removes it. That is the rule to internalise: <strong>each integer index removes an axis; each slice keeps it</strong>. <code>a[1:, ::2]</code> keeps both axes and shrinks them. Negative indices count from the end, so <code>a[-1, -1]</code> is the last element and <code>acts[:, -1]</code> is "the last position of every prompt", which is where next-token logits live.</p>
<p>Two more index forms will appear constantly. A boolean mask of the same shape selects elements into a flat array. An integer list, <code>a[[0, 2]]</code>, picks rows and keeps the axis. And <code>None</code> in an index inserts a new axis of size 1 at that position: <code>a[None]</code> is <code>(1, 3, 4)</code>, <code>a[:, None]</code> is <code>(3, 1, 4)</code>. You will use that to turn a single prompt into a batch of one, and in broadcasting next lesson.</p>
<h2 id="views">Slices are views</h2>
<p><code>row = a[0]</code> did not copy. Writing <code>row[0] = 99</code> changed <code>a[0, 0]</code>, because basic slicing returns a view onto the same memory. This is lesson 02 again with arrays: a view is an alias. <code>a[0].copy()</code> is the way to get your own memory. Boolean and integer-list indexing, by contrast, always copy, which is one more reason to know which kind of index you wrote.</p>
<p><code>a.reshape(-1)</code> flattens, with <code>-1</code> meaning "whatever is left". <code>a.T</code> transposes, and the output shows it is not contiguous: the elements are the same, visited in a different order. Most of the time that does not matter; it matters when a library asks for a contiguous buffer, and it is why torch's <code>.view</code> can fail where <code>.reshape</code> works (lesson 09).</p>
<h2 id="dtype">dtype decides what you get</h2>
<p><code>np.arange(12)</code> gives <code>int64</code>; dividing by 2 gives <code>float64</code>. A <code>float32</code> array plus a Python float stays <code>float32</code>, but plus a <code>float64</code> array it promotes to <code>float64</code>. Model activations are <code>float32</code> by default in torch and you want them to stay that way: a stray <code>float64</code> NumPy array doubles your memory and can make a later <code>torch.from_numpy</code> produce a tensor the model refuses to multiply with. The <code>int8</code> overflow line is a reminder that small integer types wrap silently; token ids are <code>int64</code> for a reason.</p>
<h2 id="read-aloud">A reading drill</h2>
<p>Given <code>acts</code> of shape <code>(2, 5, 768)</code>: <code>acts[0]</code> is <code>(5, 768)</code>, the first prompt. <code>acts[:, -1]</code> is <code>(2, 768)</code>, the last position of each prompt. <code>acts[0, -1]</code> is <code>(768,)</code>, one vector. <code>acts[..., :10]</code> is <code>(2, 5, 10)</code>: the ellipsis stands for "all the leading axes". <code>acts[0, :, None]</code> is <code>(5, 1, 768)</code>. If any of those took more than a second, do the practice below until none does.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In eval and interpretability work</span><p>"Take the activation at the last token" is <code>acts[:, -1]</code>. Writing <code>acts[-1]</code> instead takes the last prompt, every position, and the shapes <code>(2, 768)</code> versus <code>(5, 768)</code> are different enough that an assert catches it and similar enough that a plot does not.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Modifying a slice you thought was a copy. <code>last = acts[:, -1]; last -= last.mean()</code> centres the last position inside the original cache. If you did not intend that, you now have a corrupted cache and no error.</p></div>''',
 '<code>x</code> has shape <code>(12, 5, 5)</code>: twelve attention heads, a 5-by-5 pattern each. What are the shapes of <code>x[3]</code>, <code>x[:, 0]</code>, <code>x[:, -1, :]</code>, <code>x[3, 1, 2]</code>, and <code>x[None, 3]</code>?',
 '<code>(5, 5)</code>: head 3. <code>(12, 5)</code>: for every head, the row for query position 0. <code>(12, 5)</code>: every head, the last query row. A scalar: one attention weight. <code>(1, 5, 5)</code>: head 3 with a batch axis in front. Each integer removes an axis, each slice keeps one, <code>None</code> adds one.',
 lab('Create <code>acts = np.arange(2 * 5 * 768).reshape(2, 5, 768)</code> at a prompt. Before typing each of the ten expressions in the reading drill and the check above, write the shape down, then confirm with <code>.shape</code>. Then write to a slice and check whether the original changed.',
 'The slices change the original; <code>.copy()</code> and fancy indexing do not. Aim for correct predictions ten out of ten, twice, on different days. Shape reading is a fluency skill, and fluency is measured by speed without errors.',
 ['python3 labs/examples/arrays.py']), ['np-broadcast'], 'numpy ndarray shape dtype index slice view copy ellipsis None axis'),

lesson(7,2,'broadcasting','Broadcasting rules, with a shape-prediction drill',
 'Broadcasting is one rule, applied from the right. Learn it as a procedure you run by hand and most shape errors become predictable.',
 '''<h2 id="the-rule">The rule, as a procedure</h2>
<p>When two arrays meet in an operation, NumPy compares their shapes element-wise starting with the trailing dimension and working left. Two dimensions are compatible when they are equal or one of them is 1; otherwise the operation raises <code>ValueError: operands could not be broadcast together</code> '''+cite('np-broadcast')+'''. Torch states the same rule and adds the case explicitly: a dimension may also be missing, in which case 1 is prepended to the shorter shape until the lengths match, and each result dimension is the larger of the two '''+cite('torch-broadcast')+'''.</p>
<div class="diagram">     a:  (2, 5, 768)         b:  (768,)          c:  (2, 1, 768)
align right:                   (1, 1, 768)           (2, 1, 768)
compare:  2 vs 1 ok → 2        5 vs 1 ok → 5        768 vs 768 ok → 768
result:   (2, 5, 768)                               (2, 5, 768)</div>
<p>Run it by hand: write the shapes right-aligned, pad the shorter with ones on the left, and go column by column. A size-1 axis is stretched, conceptually, to match; no memory is copied. Any column with two different sizes neither of which is 1 is an error, and the error message names both shapes, so read it.</p>
'''+run('python3 labs/examples/broadcast_drill.py')+'''
<h2 id="read-the-drill">Read the drill</h2>
<p><code>(4, 3) + (3,)</code>: a row added to every row. <code>(4, 3) + (4, 1)</code>: a column added to every column. <code>(4, 3) + (4,)</code> fails, and this is the one people expect to work: the 4 aligns with the 3, not with the 4, because alignment is from the right. To add a per-row value you need <code>(4, 1)</code>, which is what <code>v[:, None]</code> or <code>keepdims</code> gives you. <code>(8, 1, 6, 1) + (7, 1, 5)</code> is the textbook case where every axis stretches somewhere. <code>(12, 5, 5) + (5, 5)</code> adds one mask to every head. <code>(2, 5, 768) + (5,)</code> fails because 5 meets 768.</p>
<h2 id="keepdims">The reduction that breaks the broadcast</h2>
<p>The second half of the output is the mistake you will make most. <code>acts.mean(axis=-1)</code> drops the last axis, giving <code>(2, 3)</code>, and <code>acts - mean</code> aligns 3 against 4 and fails. With <code>keepdims=True</code> the mean is <code>(2, 3, 1)</code>, which broadcasts back against <code>(2, 3, 4)</code> exactly as intended. <code>mean[..., None]</code> does the same by hand. Centering, normalising, and standardising activations are all this pattern, and the <code>row_normalize</code> and <code>center_last_axis</code> stubs in the shape lab are its two most common instances.</p>
<h2 id="explicit">Make the intent explicit</h2>
<p>Broadcasting is silent, which is the danger. <code>(5, 768) + (768,)</code> adds a bias to every token; <code>(5, 768) + (5, 1)</code> adds a per-token scalar; both run. If you meant the other one you get a number, not an error. Three habits help. Write the shape comment next to each operand. Insert axes with <code>None</code> rather than relying on the implicit left-padding, so the alignment you intend is the one on the page. And when a result is a shape you did not predict, stop: a <code>(5, 5)</code> where you wanted <code>(5,)</code> is almost always a broadcast that produced an outer product.</p>
<p>In-place operations have one extra restriction. The in-place tensor cannot change shape as a result of the broadcast '''+cite('torch-broadcast')+''', so <code>a += b</code> works when <code>b</code> broadcasts to <code>a</code>'s shape and fails when the result would be larger than <code>a</code>.</p>
<h2 id="lab">The shape lab</h2>
<p><code>labs/shapes.py</code> has six stubs. <code>broadcast_shape(a, b)</code> is the rule above in pure Python, and its last test compares your answer with NumPy's on fifty random shape pairs. <code>row_normalize</code>, <code>center_last_axis</code> and <code>masked_mean_pool</code> are keepdims exercises. <code>pairwise_dot</code> and <code>causal_attention_pattern</code> wait for einsum in lesson 08. The tests are shape assertions first and value checks second.</p>
'''+run("python3 -m unittest discover -s labs -p 'test_shapes.py' -v 2>&1 | grep -c 'ERROR'", note='Count of erroring tests on the shipped stubs: your target is zero.')+'''
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In eval and interpretability work</span><p>Subtracting a mean activation from a cache, projecting onto a direction, and applying an attention mask to every head are all broadcasts. The linear-algebra course will make you do the first two with real GPT-2 activations; the shape rule is the same there.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Averaging over a padded batch. <code>acts.mean(axis=1)</code> on a <code>(batch, seq, d)</code> cache includes the padding positions. <code>masked_mean_pool</code> in the lab exists so you have written the correct version once: multiply by the mask with a trailing axis added, sum, and divide by the mask's count.</p></div>''',
 'You compute <code>scores = q @ k.T</code> with <code>q</code> of shape <code>(5, 64)</code> and <code>k</code> of shape <code>(5, 64)</code>, then <code>scores - scores.max(axis=-1)</code> before a softmax. The result has shape <code>(5, 5)</code> and no error. Is it right?',
 'No. <code>scores.max(axis=-1)</code> is <code>(5,)</code>, which aligns against the last axis of <code>(5, 5)</code>: each column gets a different row\'s maximum subtracted. The softmax then has the wrong stabiliser per row and, worse, a result that is subtly wrong rather than an error. <code>keepdims=True</code> gives <code>(5, 1)</code> and subtracts each row\'s own maximum.',
 lab('Implement <code>broadcast_shape</code>, <code>row_normalize</code>, <code>center_last_axis</code> and <code>masked_mean_pool</code> in <code>labs/shapes.py</code>. Run only the shape tests. Before each run, say which tests you expect to pass.',
 'The random-shape test will catch an off-by-one in your alignment that the hand-written cases miss. For <code>row_normalize</code>, the zero-row test forces you to divide only where the norm is nonzero; <code>np.divide(..., out=..., where=...)</code> or a masked assignment both work. Leave <code>pairwise_dot</code> and the attention pattern for the next lesson.',
 ["python3 -m unittest discover -s labs -p 'test_shapes.py' -v 2>&1 | grep -c 'ERROR'"]), ['np-broadcast','torch-broadcast'], 'broadcast rule keepdims None axis ValueError shape prediction drill'),

lesson(8,2,'reductions-and-einsum','Reductions, axes, and einsum read aloud',
 'A reduction names the axis it removes. An einsum string names every axis and says which ones disappear. Read both out loud and the shapes take care of themselves.',
 '''<h2 id="reductions">Reductions remove the axis you name</h2>
<p><code>acts.sum()</code> with no axis reduces everything to a scalar. <code>acts.sum(axis=0)</code> removes axis 0 and keeps the rest; on <code>(batch, seq, d)</code> that sums over prompts and leaves <code>(seq, d)</code>. The sentence to say is "sum over the batch axis", and the shape follows: the named axis disappears. <code>axis=-1</code> names the last axis, <code>axis=(0, 1)</code> names two, and <code>keepdims=True</code> keeps the removed axes as size 1 for broadcasting, as lesson 07 showed.</p>
'''+run('python3 labs/examples/reductions_einsum.py')+'''
<p><code>argmax</code> is a reduction that returns indices rather than values, with the same shape rule: <code>logits.argmax(axis=-1)</code> on <code>(batch, seq, vocab)</code> is <code>(batch, seq)</code>, the predicted token id at every position. <code>np.linalg.norm(acts, axis=-1)</code> removes <code>d</code> and leaves one length per position, which is exactly the "norm of the residual stream across layers" line you will see in lesson 12.</p>
<h2 id="einsum">einsum: name the axes, drop the ones you sum</h2>
<p>An einsum string lists one letter per axis of each operand, separated by commas, then <code>-&gt;</code> and the letters of the output. Whenever a label is repeated across operands it is summed over; a label that appears once is kept; in explicit mode the output labels control exactly which axes survive and in what order '''+cite('np-einsum')+'''. That is the whole notation. The practice is reading it aloud.</p>
<table><tr><th>String</th><th>Read aloud</th><th>Shapes</th><th>Same as</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>sd,d-&gt;s</code></td><td>for each position s, sum over d of x[s,d]·v[d]</td><td>(5,768),(768,)→(5,)</td><td><code>x @ v</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>sd,td-&gt;st</code></td><td>for each s and t, sum over d</td><td>(5,768),(7,768)→(5,7)</td><td><code>x @ y.T</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>bhqd,bhkd-&gt;bhqk</code></td><td>per batch and head, every query against every key, sum over d</td><td>(2,12,5,64)²→(2,12,5,5)</td><td><code>q @ k.transpose(-1,-2)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>bsd-&gt;bd</code></td><td>sum over positions</td><td>(2,3,4)→(2,4)</td><td><code>acts.sum(axis=1)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>bsd,de-&gt;bse</code></td><td>apply a (768→3072) weight to every token</td><td>(2,5,768),(768,3072)→(2,5,3072)</td><td><code>acts @ W</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ii-&gt;i</code></td><td>the diagonal</td><td>(3,3)→(3,)</td><td><code>np.diag</code></td></tr></table>
<p>The attention line is the one to memorise. Queries and keys share the batch and head axes, which are kept; they share <code>d</code>, which is summed; the query and key position axes get different letters because they must both survive, and the output order <code>bhqk</code> says "for each query, a row over keys". The <code>causal_attention_pattern</code> stub is this string followed by a scale, a mask, and a softmax over the last axis.</p>
<h2 id="why-einsum">Why reach for einsum instead of @</h2>
<p>Matrix multiply works on the last two axes and broadcasts the rest, which is fine until you need to contract a middle axis or keep an axis that <code>@</code> would merge. einsum makes the contraction explicit and self-documenting: six months later, <code>bhqd,bhkd-&gt;bhqk</code> still says what it does, while <code>q @ k.transpose(-1, -2)</code> requires you to recall which axes <code>transpose</code> swapped. Torch has <code>torch.einsum</code> with the same strings, and the <code>einops</code> library extends the idea to reshapes; TransformerLens code uses both heavily, so reading them is a prerequisite for reading the toolkit.</p>
<p>Two reading rules for other people's strings. A letter repeated inside one operand, <code>ii</code>, takes the diagonal. Implicit mode, a string without <code>-&gt;</code>, orders the output alphabetically, which is why <code>np.einsum('ji', a)</code> is a transpose; prefer explicit mode in anything you write.</p>
<h2 id="lab">Finish the shape lab</h2>
<p><code>pairwise_dot</code> is the second row of the table. <code>causal_attention_pattern</code> is the third row, then divide by <code>sqrt(d_head)</code>, set every entry with key position after query position to minus infinity, subtract the row maximum with <code>keepdims</code>, exponentiate, and divide by the row sum. The tests check that rows sum to one, that the upper triangle is exactly zero, that the scale is <code>sqrt(64)</code>, and that scores of <code>1e4</code> do not overflow to NaN, which is what the row-maximum subtraction is for.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In eval and interpretability work</span><p>Projecting a <code>(batch, seq, 768)</code> cache onto a <code>(768,)</code> direction is <code>bsd,d-&gt;bs</code>. Comparing every token's activation with every other's is <code>sd,td-&gt;st</code>. An interpretability result is usually one of these two contractions followed by a claim, and the string is the clearest statement of what was computed.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Summing over an axis you meant to keep because two operands used the same letter by accident. <code>bsd,bsd-&gt;b</code> sums over positions and features; <code>bsd,bsd-&gt;bs</code> keeps positions. Both run. Read the output letters before you trust the number.</p></div>''',
 'Read <code>np.einsum("bsd,bd-&gt;bs", acts, v)</code> aloud, give its shape for <code>acts</code> <code>(2, 5, 768)</code> and <code>v</code> <code>(2, 768)</code>, and say what it computes.',
 'For each batch item b and position s, the sum over d of acts[b,s,d]·v[b,d]: the dot product of every position with that prompt\'s own direction vector. Shape <code>(2, 5)</code>. Note the difference from <code>bsd,d-&gt;bs</code>, which would use one direction for both prompts.',
 lab('Implement <code>pairwise_dot</code> and <code>causal_attention_pattern</code>, make the whole shape lab green, then write the einsum string for "the attention-weighted sum of values": pattern <code>(b, h, q, k)</code> with values <code>(b, h, k, d)</code>.',
 '<code>bhqk,bhkd-&gt;bhqd</code>: sum over keys, keep queries. Check it against <code>pattern @ v</code>. When the lab is green, run it one more time with <code>-v</code> and read each test name as a sentence; those sentences are the spec you have now satisfied.',
 ["python3 -m unittest discover -s labs -p 'test_shapes.py' 2>&1 | tail -1"]), ['np-einsum'], 'sum mean axis argmax norm einsum subscripts contraction attention'),

lesson(9,2,'torch-tensors','torch tensors: dtype, device, views versus copies, in-place ops, no_grad',
 'A tensor is a NumPy array with three extra facts attached: a device, a dtype that is enforced rather than promoted, and an optional autograd history.',
 '''<h2 id="three-facts">The three facts to check</h2>
<p>Every tensor answers <code>t.shape</code>, <code>t.dtype</code>, and <code>t.device</code>. Shape works as in NumPy; <code>torch.Size</code> is a tuple subclass, so <code>t.shape == (3, 4)</code> is a valid comparison and <code>t.shape[-1]</code> is the last size. The default float dtype is <code>float32</code>, not NumPy's <code>float64</code>, and models are stored in it. The device is <code>cpu</code> throughout this course; on a GPU box you move tensors with <code>.to("cuda")</code> and must move them back with <code>.cpu()</code> before <code>.numpy()</code>.</p>
'''+run('python3 labs/examples/tensors.py')+'''
<h2 id="dtype">dtype is enforced</h2>
<p>NumPy promoted <code>float32</code> plus <code>float64</code> to <code>float64</code>. Torch raises for a <code>float32</code> matrix product with a <code>float64</code> operand, as the third line shows. This is the right behaviour for model code, where an accidental promotion would double memory and halve speed, but it means a NumPy array you convert with <code>torch.from_numpy</code> arrives as <code>float64</code> if that is what NumPy made, and the model refuses it. Convert with <code>.float()</code> or <code>.to(torch.float32)</code>, and keep <code>.half()</code> for when you know the model was trained or quantised in half precision.</p>
<h2 id="views">Views, contiguity, and the two reshape methods</h2>
<p>A view shares the underlying data with its base tensor, so writing through the view changes the base '''+cite('torch-views')+'''. <code>a.view(6)</code> above is a view: <code>a[0, 0]</code> became 7 and <code>data_ptr()</code> is the same address. <code>transpose</code>, <code>permute</code>, <code>squeeze</code>, and basic slicing return views too. A view of a contiguous tensor can be non-contiguous; the transpose shows <code>is_contiguous()</code> false, and <code>.view</code> then fails because it needs a contiguous buffer. <code>.reshape</code> returns a view when it can and a copy when it must, so code should not assume either '''+cite('torch-views')+'''; <code>.contiguous()</code> returns the tensor itself if already contiguous and a copy otherwise.</p>
<p>The practical rule: use <code>.reshape</code> unless you specifically need the guarantee that no copy happened, and call <code>.clone()</code> on anything you cache. Lesson 13's hooks do exactly that, because the alternative is a cache that aliases the model's working memory.</p>
<h2 id="inplace">In-place operations</h2>
<p>A trailing underscore means in place: <code>b.add_(1)</code> changes <code>b</code>'s storage and returns it. Every alias sees it; <code>c.mul_(2)</code> doubled <code>b</code>. In-place ops save memory and are everywhere in model code (<code>x.masked_fill_</code>, <code>param.data.zero_()</code>), and they have two costs. They interact badly with autograd, which may need the overwritten value for a gradient and will raise if a saved tensor was modified '''+cite('torch-autograd')+'''. And they cannot change shape through a broadcast. Default to the out-of-place form and reach for the underscore when you have measured a need.</p>
<p><code>torch.from_numpy</code> shares memory with the array, and <code>.numpy()</code> shares it back: changing one changes the other, as the <code>n[0] = 5</code> line shows. That is a view across libraries, and the same aliasing rules apply.</p>
<h2 id="no-grad">requires_grad, no_grad, and detach</h2>
<p><code>w = torch.ones(3, requires_grad=True)</code> asks autograd to record operations on <code>w</code>. The result <code>y</code> then has <code>requires_grad</code> true and a <code>grad_fn</code>, the node that knows how to differentiate a sum. Inside <code>torch.no_grad()</code> the same computation is not recorded: <code>z.grad_fn</code> is <code>None</code>. The autograd notes describe no-grad mode as making computations behave as if none of the inputs require grad, and recommend it for operations that should not be recorded '''+cite('torch-autograd')+'''. Every forward pass in this course whose gradients you do not need runs under it, which is all of them except the fit in lesson 10.</p>
<p><code>detach()</code> returns a tensor sharing data with the original but cut from the graph. It is what you call on a captured activation before storing it, and before <code>.numpy()</code>: the output shows <code>w.numpy()</code> refusing because the tensor requires grad, and <code>w.detach().numpy()</code> working. <code>.item()</code> extracts a one-element tensor as a Python number, and <code>.tolist()</code> converts a whole tensor; both are how a tensor becomes something <code>json.dumps</code> accepts.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In eval and interpretability work</span><p>A cache that holds tensors with <code>requires_grad=True</code> keeps every intermediate of the forward pass alive through their <code>grad_fn</code> chains. The memory does not come back until the cache is dropped, and <code>torch.save</code> of such a tensor is larger and slower. <code>no_grad</code> on the way in and <code>detach().clone()</code> on the way out is the whole discipline.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Calling <code>.view(-1)</code> on a transposed or permuted tensor, getting a <code>RuntimeError</code> about strides, and "fixing" it by sprinkling <code>.contiguous()</code> everywhere. Understand why the tensor was non-contiguous first; usually a <code>permute</code> produced it and <code>.reshape</code> is the honest call.</p></div>''',
 'A hook stores <code>output</code> directly in a dict during a forward pass under <code>torch.no_grad()</code>. Later, a different part of the code runs <code>x.add_(bias)</code> on what turns out to be the same storage. Which lesson-02 rule applies, and what should the hook have done?',
 'Aliasing: the dict holds a reference to the model\'s tensor, not a copy, so the in-place add changes the cache after the fact. The hook should store <code>output.detach().clone()</code>. <code>no_grad</code> solved the autograd half of the problem, not the identity half; they are separate.',
 lab('At a prompt, build <code>t = torch.arange(6.0).reshape(2, 3)</code>. Predict and then check: whether <code>t.t()</code> is contiguous; whether <code>t.t().view(6)</code> raises; whether <code>t.t().reshape(6)</code> shares storage with <code>t</code> (<code>data_ptr()</code>); and whether <code>t[0].add_(1)</code> changes <code>t</code>.',
 'Not contiguous; raises; does not share storage because reshape had to copy; and yes, because <code>t[0]</code> is a view and <code>add_</code> writes through it. If any prediction was wrong, re-read the views section before moving on; lesson 13 relies on all four.',
 ['python3 labs/examples/tensors.py']), ['torch-views','torch-autograd'], 'tensor dtype device view reshape contiguous in-place no_grad detach numpy item'),

lesson(10,2,'autograd','Autograd: what a gradient is operationally, backward(), and a tiny fit',
 'You will rarely train anything in interpretability work, but you will read gradients, attributions, and optimisers constantly. Here is the mechanism in forty lines.',
 '''<h2 id="operationally">A gradient, operationally</h2>
<p>For a scalar loss and a parameter <code>w</code>, the gradient <code>dloss/dw</code> is how much the loss changes per small change in <code>w</code>. The script below checks that definition directly: it nudges <code>w</code> by <code>1e-3</code>, recomputes the loss, and divides the change by the nudge. The finite difference and the autograd value agree to three decimals. That is what <code>backward()</code> computes, for every parameter at once, without the nudging.</p>
'''+run('python3 labs/examples/autograd_fit.py')+'''
<h2 id="mechanism">The mechanism</h2>
<p>A tensor with <code>requires_grad=True</code> is a leaf. An operation is recorded in the backward graph only if at least one of its inputs requires grad '''+cite('torch-autograd')+'''; the result carries a <code>grad_fn</code> that points back at the inputs. <code>loss.backward()</code> walks that graph from the loss to the leaves, applying the chain rule, and accumulates the result into each leaf's <code>.grad</code>. Only leaf tensors with <code>requires_grad=True</code> get a <code>.grad</code> field filled '''+cite('torch-autograd')+'''; intermediates do not keep theirs. The first line of output is the gradient at <code>w = 0, b = 0</code>: both negative, meaning increasing either parameter would decrease the loss, which is why the update subtracts the gradient.</p>
<h2 id="loop">The training loop, line by line</h2>
<p><code>w.grad = None</code> clears the previous gradient. The last line of the output shows what happens without that: two <code>backward()</code> calls accumulate to 4, not 2, because <code>.grad</code> is summed into, not replaced. Every optimiser's <code>zero_grad()</code> exists for this reason. Then the forward pass builds a fresh graph, <code>backward()</code> fills <code>.grad</code>, and the update <code>w -= lr * w.grad</code> runs inside <code>torch.no_grad()</code> because the update must not itself be recorded; if it were, the next backward would try to differentiate through the optimiser step, and in-place modification of a leaf that requires grad is an error outside no-grad mode. After 200 steps the line is <code>3.03x + 0.99</code> against a truth of <code>3x + 1</code> plus noise.</p>
<p>That loop is <code>torch.optim.SGD</code> with the optimiser inlined. Real code replaces the three middle lines with <code>opt.zero_grad(); loss.backward(); opt.step()</code>, and the mechanism is the same. When you read a training script you are reading this loop plus batching.</p>
<h2 id="where-you-will-meet-it">Where interpretability meets autograd</h2>
<p>Attribution methods differentiate a logit with respect to an activation: <code>logit.backward()</code> after enabling grad on a cached residual-stream tensor gives "how much would this logit move per unit change in each of the 768 features here". Integrated gradients, attribution patching, and gradient-based feature visualisation all rest on exactly the mechanism in this script, with the model's own parameters frozen (<code>requires_grad=False</code>) and the activation or input made a leaf. The interpretability course uses it; this lesson is so that nothing in it will look like magic.</p>
<p>The flip side is the cost. A forward pass that records a graph keeps every intermediate alive for the backward pass. For GPT-2 small that is tens of megabytes per short prompt, which you pay for nothing when you only wanted activations. <code>torch.no_grad()</code> for inference, <code>requires_grad_(True)</code> on the one tensor you want gradients for, and <code>detach()</code> on the way out: the three habits from lesson 09, now with the reason.</p>
<h2 id="checks">Two sanity checks to keep</h2>
<p>The finite-difference check in the script is cheap and general: if an attribution number looks odd, nudge the input and see whether the loss moves the way the gradient said. And the shape check: a gradient has the shape of the thing it is with respect to. <code>acts.grad</code> for a <code>(1, 5, 768)</code> activation is <code>(1, 5, 768)</code>. If you ever see a gradient of a different shape you have differentiated with respect to something else.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In eval and interpretability work</span><p>"Attribution" is a gradient times something. When a paper reports which tokens or features mattered for a prediction, ask what scalar was differentiated, with respect to which tensor, and whether the parameters were frozen. Those three answers are three lines of code, and you can now read them.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Calling <code>backward()</code> on a non-scalar. Autograd needs a scalar to start from; <code>logits.backward()</code> raises, and the fix is to choose the scalar you mean, such as <code>logits[0, -1, token_id]</code>, rather than <code>.sum()</code>, which answers a different question.</p></div>''',
 'The script\'s gradient at the start is about <code>-3.0</code> for <code>w</code> and <code>-5.0</code> for <code>b</code>. Why is the gradient for <code>b</code> larger in magnitude when the true slope is larger than the true intercept?',
 'The gradient measures sensitivity of the loss, not the size of the parameter. With <code>x</code> in [0, 1], the mean of <code>x</code> is 0.5, so a unit change in <code>w</code> shifts predictions by 0.5 on average while a unit change in <code>b</code> shifts them by 1. The loss is therefore about twice as sensitive to <code>b</code>, and the ratio is close to 5:3 rather than 2:1 because the residuals are not uniform. Sensitivity, not value.',
 lab('Edit a copy of <code>autograd_fit.py</code> to remove the <code>w.grad = None; b.grad = None</code> line and run it. Then restore it and instead remove the <code>torch.no_grad()</code> around the update. Explain each failure from the output.',
 'Without clearing, gradients accumulate across steps, the effective learning rate grows every step, and the fit diverges or oscillates. Without <code>no_grad</code>, torch raises because an in-place operation on a leaf that requires grad is not allowed to be recorded; the error message says so. Both are the two most common bugs in hand-written training loops.',
 ['python3 labs/examples/autograd_fit.py']), ['torch-autograd'], 'gradient backward requires_grad leaf grad accumulate optimizer finite difference attribution'),
]
